Final Data Essay: How much should you write?

Target: approximately 1,500 words
Maximum: 1,750 words
References, captions, tables, and code do not count.


The final data essay should be focused rather than comprehensive. Students do not need to narrate every step or reproduce all their documentation. Complete datasets, code, notebooks, and detailed reuse guidance belong in GitHub.
The essay should use the available space to make a coherent argument connecting the custom dataset and the audit. Prioritize:

at least two concrete examples from the student’s own data;
a meaningful methodological decision, revision, or failure;
direct reference to code, a computational result, table, or visualization;
an important limitation or unresolved uncertainty;
a concise explanation of any AI use and how its output was checked.

The word limit excludes references, figure captions, tables, and code. Longer essays are not necessarily stronger. The goal is specificity, selection, and a well-supported argument rather than broad background or repeated description.

What Does “Most Popular” Mean on TikTok?







McNulty opens with Zach King’s “Magic Ride,” which multiple sources identified as the most-viewed TikTok. It reportedly received more than two billion views during its first four days online.
“That makes it, arguably, one of the most visible new aesthetic creations in the contemporary world.” (McNulty, 2023, p. 3)
She compares this scale with film ticket sales and New York Times bestseller sales, while warning that all these numbers can be inflated or calculated differently. The comparison is not meant to prove that a TikTok is more culturally important than a novel or film. It demonstrates the enormous scale of platform circulation and the inadequacy of many inherited measures of cultural significance.
Before accepting a popularity metric, I want to ask what action it counts, who performed that action, over what interval, and whether repeated or automated activity is included. A view is neither a person nor necessarily a meaningful encounter.
“Of the 61 creators’ whose work, produced in 2022, is here represented, the two most followed—consistently vying for the top spot—are Khaby Lame and Charli D’Amelio. (Zach King, meanwhile, hovers around sixth place). Lame is an Italian creator (Senegalese-born), who makes videos that mock other popular TikToks, usually by revealing that their “lifehacks” actually overcomplicate simple processes: in one, typical of the genre, he first replays another creator’s performance of removing the skin from a banana, elaborately; he then peels a banana normally, wearing his signature deflationary expression” (McNulty, 2023, p. 10)
“Lame, in other words, is one of TikTok’s many bubble bursters: creators who comment, in meta-fashion, on other TikToks, often via the “stitch” function, which allows them to fuse their own videos with prior TikToks (WonJeong is another especially popular creator who specializes in these videos). D’Amelio, meanwhile, is one of the platform’s many sirens: scantily clad dancers who wink and wiggle their way through its frenetic feeds (Kimberly Loaiza is another, with a particularly devoted following). Both Lame and D’Amelio now have 150 million plus separate followers: a group about half the size of the US population.” (McNulty, 2023, p. 10)
“Here are some of the trends that I’ve encountered, across the popular TikToks in my collection. Collectively, the most commonly used caption-words included (with or without hashtags): “tiktokfood,” “ASMR,” “love,” “mom,” “girlfriend,” “sister,” “dance,” “happy,” “think,” “time,” “today,” and the tearful laughter emoji (many other top words were in Spanish, Korean, or platform-specific lingo. Though 50 out of 61 of the accounts were entirely in English, some of the non-English accounts produced particularly large quantities of videos).” (McNulty, 2023, p. 12)

music performance videos, including dance, lip-synch, or scenes playacted along with music (42% of the TikToks);
autobiographical, or more ostensibly “real” content, presenting scenes from life or personal opinions, or communicating with fans (36%);
stunt, mime, or illusion videos, featuring physical feats, and bodily altering performances, potentially involving animation or cosplay (22%);
skit videos, presenting fictional scenes (20%);
materially oriented content, foregrounding physical, object-centric processes, like crafts, cooking, makeup application, et cetera (15%);
media-commentary Tiktoks, responding to other Tiktoks or entertainment (10%);
overt brand advertisements, often as enfolded into other categories (1%);
(and then, of course, other miscellany).

