    &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
"""

soup = BeautifulSoup(html_doc, "html.parser")
print(soup.prettify())


This is the small example from Beautiful Soup’s Quick Start and the lesson. It lets us practice parsing before adding the complication of downloading a live page.
The triple quotes create a multiline string. Beautiful Soup parses that string and returns a BeautifulSoup object. .prettify() produces an indented representation of the parsed tree so we can inspect how the elements nest.
I can ask students to identify the root html element, the head and body, the two paragraphs, and the three links. This should connect directly to their earlier work writing HTML.
prettify() is useful for inspection, but it is not the extraction step. The next task is to search the tree for specific elements.

